# Code structure (RTDMS)

Applies to every zone (app/, pi/, fusion/, models/, tools). Reviewers and delegates are held to it.

## Reuse first, always

1. **Search before writing.** Before adding a function, component, hook, type, schema or script,
   grep the repo (and `contracts/`, `app/src/components/`, `app/src/theme/`, `models/*/src/`,
   `fusion/`) for an existing one. Reuse or extend it. Name what you reused in the PR body.
2. **One source of truth per fact.** Payload shapes live in `contracts/pi-link/schemas/`; tokens in
   `app/src/theme/`; alert patterns in `fusion/` (`AlertPattern`); UUIDs in one constants module.
   Never copy a value into a second file: import it or generate it.
3. **Do not fork or copy other zones' code.** Import it (e.g. `pi/` imports `models/` and `fusion/`).
   If it needs a change, ask that zone's owner or open a CR/PR there.
4. **No new dependency** if the standard library or an existing dependency does the job.

## Small, single-purpose files

- **Code files: target ≤ 250 lines, hard limit 300** (tests may reach 400). Functions/components
  ≤ 50 lines. Split by responsibility (parse / state / transport / view), not by arbitrary length.
- **No grab-bag modules** (`utils.ts`, `helpers.py`, `common.py`). Name files for what they do.
- **One screen = a thin route file + a screen folder** (`screens/<name>/index.tsx` composing small
  components and a hook). Business logic stays out of components (services/hooks), I/O stays out of
  pure logic (the crash detector and parsers stay pure and unit-tested).
- **Docs: ≤ 400 lines per note.** Split longer ones into linked notes with an index; do not pile
  everything into one file.

## Patterns (use the one that fits; do not invent frameworks)

- **Strategy / interface** for swappable I/O: `PiTransport` (HTTP, BLE, demo) on the app side,
  `Source` protocols on the Pi side. Callers depend on the interface, never on a concrete class.
- **Pure core, thin shell:** decisions (fusion, crash detection, parsing) are pure functions over
  data; sockets, GPIO, BLE, files and the clock are injected at the edge. This is what makes them
  testable without hardware.
- **State machines as data** for lifecycles (a transition table + a reducer), not nested `if`s.
- **Dependency injection by parameter**, not module-level singletons or hidden globals.
- **Validate at the boundary** (schemas for payloads, parsers returning `null` on bad input) and
  trust internal types after that.
- **Design-system components** for UI: no hex, font size or spacing literal in a screen file;
  compose existing components before adding one; add a component only when 2+ screens need it.

## Gates that enforce this (run them; do not just claim it)

- Longest-files check: `git ls-files '*.ts' '*.tsx' '*.py' | xargs wc -l | sort -rn | head` and
  list any file over 300 lines in the PR body with the reason (or split it).
- Reuse check in the PR body: "Reused: …" and "New and why: …".
- App: `npx tsc --noEmit && npx eslint . && npm test` plus the design-token grep in
  `app/docs/VALIDATION.md`. Pi: `ruff`, `mypy --strict`, `pytest` in Docker.
